@flexiarg baldwin/test-evolution-of-learnability-before-static-assimilation
@title Test Evolution of Learnability Before Complete Assimilation
@sigils [🎑/力]
@keywords metaca, learnability, lifetime-learning, early-performance, ablation, preregistration
@audience MetaCA experiment designers, evolutionary-computation researchers
@tone prospective
@style pattern
@childof baldwin/mechanism
@references [baldwin/two-claims-not-one baldwin/plasticity-builds-a-selective-neighbourhood baldwin/stationarity-decides-what-can-be-fixed]
@references-extra ["Gupta et al. (2021), doi:10.1038/s41586-021-03522-2" "Wong et al. (2023), arXiv:2312.03243"]

! conclusion:
  The next MetaCA Baldwin experiment should first ask whether lifetime rewriting causes
  evolution of inherited structures that learn faster or generalize better; complete
  static assimilation should remain a stronger secondary endpoint.

  + context:
    Modern computational demonstrations often evolve morphology, initialization, or
    hyperparameters that facilitate subsequent learning. Gupta et al. report evolved
    morphologies that learn control earlier and more efficiently; Wong et al. evolve
    inherited PINN structure over a task distribution while task-specific output
    learning remains within lifetime.

  + IF:
    MetaCA supplies heritable initial rule fields and rewrite parameters, a lifetime
    trajectory, and reproducible task distributions.

  + HOWEVER:
    Selecting only final reach favors strong rewriting but does not distinguish a genome
    that learns quickly from one rescued late. Charging a symbolic `gamma * not-held`
    proxy can suppress plasticity without proving that evolution has internalized any
    function.

  + THEN:
    Cross lifetime rewriting enabled versus disabled during evolution while keeping
    genetic randomness and evaluation tasks paired. Score raw function together with
    time-to-threshold, cumulative realized rewrites, and held-out-task performance.
    After evolution, evaluate ancestral and evolved genotypes under truncated learning
    budgets and no-learning ablation. Register guidance if evolved inherited structure
    shifts the learning curve relative to the no-learning evolutionary control; register
    assimilation only if dependence also falls without functional loss.

    + CHECK:
      Use multiple preregistered evolution seeds; fixed training and held-out task sets;
      aligned draw schedules; treatment-separation checks; mutation-only nulls; and a
      positive control reproducing the Hinton--Nowlan selective-neighbourhood mechanism.

  + BECAUSE:
    Learning-guided evolution predicts a change in inherited preparedness. Learning
    curves and ablations observe preparedness directly, whereas final reach and hold
    count confound it with the amount of plastic work performed.

    + COUNTERFACTUAL:
      If evolved and ancestral genotypes have indistinguishable learning curves under
      paired tasks, high final reach is ordinary plastic competence rather than evidence
      that learning guided evolution.

  + NEXT-STEPS:
    - Encode the guidance outcome and its falsifier in ExperimentPreregistration.
    - Run the positive control and response-curve preflight before provisioning.
